  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't confirmed the dashboard's panels render with data or that the rule has run yet. The rule's status is still \"pending\".\n\n**What the data holds:** Each document is a web request. The fields I used are `timestamp`, `bytes`, `response` (a string, so I used `response.keyword`) and `geo.src`. Other fields include `extension`, `host`, `clientip`, `machine.os` and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard \"EFF-40959e52 Web logs overview\"** (5 panels):\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Requests over time (area)\n- Requests by response code (pie)\n- Average bytes over time (line)\n- Top source countries (bar)\n\nIt's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-40959e52-web-logs-overview-9940357c1255.\n\n**Rule \"EFF-40959e52 5xx spike\"** (id `fd4ebee8-be0b-4085-8818-2d5be0d73463`):\n- It is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes.\n- It fires when that count is above 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so it creates alerts in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone.",
